## Lane B — the anchored short-window prime target and the exponent it needs

New file `RiemannAnalytic/ShortWindowPrimeTarget.lean`.

### The narrower sufficient target, formalized

`finiteGridAnchorMinorSum φ h α m` is the anchored coordinate observable of the
one-sided source window: the `m` squared `2×2` minors through the central grid
point.  Proved:

* `finiteGridAnchorMinorSum_eq` — it equals `a₀²‖b‖²` (the transverse coordinate
  vanishes at the anchor for a real even taper);
* `finiteGridAnchorMinorSum_le_gramDefect` — it is dominated by the full window
  Gram defect, so any `e₂`-budget is a budget for it;
* `anchoredMinorSum_source_floor` — **it carries the entire source floor by
  itself**: `(9/16)L²((9/512)L⁴α² − tail) ≤ finiteGridAnchorMinorSum`;
* `anchored_floor_family`, `anchored_budget_tube` — a prime-side bound on the
  *anchored* weighted sum alone already yields
  `|α_ρ| ≤ √(E/((81/16384)L⁶))`.

So the arithmetic side never has to reconstruct the local `e₂`: the `O(q)`
anchored minors suffice, exactly as conjectured.

### What cancellation exponent in the window height is required

With a mesoscopic window of height `H`, local zero count `≍ A·H·L` and the true
per-vector energy `≍ C_b L²` (`LocalTraceScale.lean`), the norm-and-count budget
is `mesoBudget A C_b L H θ`, where `H^θ` records an assumed arithmetic saving.

* `mesoBudget_ratio_eq` — `mesoBudget / L⁶ = 2A²C_b²·H^{2−θ}`;
* `meso_no_gain_of_exponent_le_two` — for `θ ≤ 2` and any window with `H ≥ 1`,
  the normalized budget stays `≥ 2A²C_b²`: never `o(L⁶)`;
* `meso_bounded_window_no_gain` — for a *fixed* height it is a nonzero constant,
  so bounded height by itself never produces the required `o(L⁶)`;
* `meso_gain_of_exponent_gt_two` — for `θ > 2` with `H → ∞` it does tend to `0`.

**The exponent question is therefore answered at the scaling level: a
short-interval input is useful here only if it beats the trivial count by more
than the square of the window height.**

### The short-window second moment itself

New file `RiemannAnalytic/ShortIntervalMeanValue.lean` proves, from scratch, the
identity every short-interval mean-value theorem starts from.  For
`D(t) = ∑_{1≤n≤N} a_n e^{−it log n}`:

* `integral_expWindow` — `∫₀^H e^{iλt} dt = H` (diagonal) or `(e^{iλH}−1)/(iλ)`;
* `dirichletPoly_secondMoment_eq` — the exact expansion
  `∫₀^H |D|² = ∑_{m,n} a_m conj(a_n)·expWindow H (log n − log m)`;
* `norm_expWindow_le` — off the diagonal, `‖expWindow H λ‖ ≤ 2/|λ|`,
  **uniformly in `H`**;
* `dirichletPoly_secondMoment_le` — hence
  `∫₀^H |D|² ≤ H ∑|a_n|² + ∑_{m≠n} 2|a_m||a_n|/|log n − log m|`.

The last two together are the quantitative form of the difficulty: the diagonal
scales with the window, the classical off-diagonal cost does not.  In the
language above, the classical expansion supplies cancellation exponent `θ = 0`,
whereas the local exterior-area target needs `θ > 2`.  A usable short-interval
input must beat this expansion (Montgomery–Vaughan-type or short-interval
mean-value theory), not merely invoke it; no such input is assumed anywhere in
this project.

